L["thorns"] = """The night Jesus was arrested, he was brought before the Jewish council and then taken to the Roman governor, Pontius Pilate. Pilate questioned him and said he found no basis for a charge. But the crowd, stirred up by the religious leaders, kept demanding that Jesus be put to death. Pilate was afraid of losing control. Then Pilate took Jesus and had him flogged.

The soldiers of the governor then took Jesus into the Praetorium and gathered the whole company of soldiers around him. They stripped him and put a scarlet robe on him. Then they twisted together a crown of thorns and set it on his head. They put a staff in his right hand. Kneeling in front of him, they mocked him. Hail, king of the Jews! they said. They spit on him, and took the staff and struck him on the head again and again. After they had mocked him, they took off the robe and put his own clothes on him.

This is hard to read. The soldiers were treating Jesus as a joke, a pretend king. They wanted to humiliate him. But what they did was more true than they knew. He was a king, and he was wearing a crown. The thorns were a sign of the curse that came into the world through human sin. In Genesis, God said that the ground would produce thorns and thistles. Jesus took the curse on his own head.

John adds, once more Pilate came out and said to the Jews gathered there, look, I am bringing him out to you to let you know that I find no basis for a charge against him. Jesus came out wearing the crown of thorns and the purple robe.

Why does this matter? The prophet Isaiah had written about a servant who would be despised and rejected, a man of suffering. He was pierced for our transgressions, he was crushed for our iniquities. By his wounds we are healed. Jesus did not endure this because he was weak. He had the power to stop it, and he stayed. He endured it for the sake of the people who mocked him.

What happened after? Jesus was led out to be crucified, and it was a crown of thorns that he wore on the road. The kingdom he came to establish was not like the kingdoms of this world. He had told Pilate, my kingdom is not of this world. If it were, my servants would fight to prevent my arrest. A kingdom built on love, forgiveness and sacrifice looked like weakness, but it has changed the world.

What does this mean for us today? When we feel mocked or humiliated, we can remember that Jesus has been there. He understands. He wasn't angry at his tormentors. Later he would pray, Father, forgive them. And the crown of thorns invites us to look at what real kingship is. It isn't force or fame. It is a willingness to give yourself for others.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus took our shame and our curse on himself, and that the King of kings was crowned with thorns for us."""

L["ecce-homo"] = """Pilate had tried several ways to avoid condemning Jesus. He had questioned him, sent him to Herod, and offered to release him in place of a prisoner. Now he brought Jesus out in front of the crowd, wearing the crown of thorns and the purple robe. Pilate said to them, here is the man! In Latin, ecce homo. Behold the man.

He may have hoped the sight of Jesus would stir pity. The man in front of them was bruised and bleeding and exhausted. But when the chief priests and their officials saw him, they shouted, crucify! Crucify! Pilate said, you take him and crucify him. As for me, I find no basis for a charge against him.

The Jewish leaders insisted, we have a law, and according to that law he must die, because he claimed to be the Son of God. When Pilate heard this, he was even more afraid. He took Jesus back inside the palace and asked him, where do you come from? But Jesus gave him no answer. Do you refuse to speak to me? Pilate said. Don't you realize I have power either to free you or to crucify you? Jesus answered, you would have no power over me if it were not given to you from above.

From then on, Pilate tried to set Jesus free, but the crowd kept shouting, if you let this man go, you are no friend of Caesar. Anyone who claims to be a king opposes Caesar. When Pilate heard this, he brought Jesus out. Here is your king, Pilate said. Take him away! Crucify him! shouted the crowd. Shall I crucify your king? Pilate asked. We have no king but Caesar, the chief priests answered. Finally Pilate handed Jesus over to them to be crucified.

Why does this matter? Pilate said more than he knew when he said, behold the man. Here was the true human being, the one who showed what humanity was made to be, and he was being rejected by the people he came to save. Pilate also illustrates a danger. He knew that Jesus was innocent, and he still gave in to pressure.

What happened after? Jesus was led away, carrying his cross. The words that Pilate spoke were remembered. Later, he even had a sign written and placed on the cross that said, Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews. It was written in three languages, so that everyone could read it. The chief priests protested, but Pilate said, what I have written, I have written.

What does this mean for us today? We are all asked to look at Jesus and decide what to do with him. Pilate tried to stay neutral, but there is no neutral. We also learn how easy it is to know what is right and still choose what is convenient, out of fear of the crowd. And the phrase, behold the man, still invites us to look at Jesus and see what love looks like when it costs everything.

That is why this story is so important. It says that Jesus was innocent, and that he accepted rejection so that we could be accepted. Behold the man. Behold the Lamb of God."""
